`KinematicLimits::operator==` (common/src/kinematic_limits.cpp:37–45) compares `joint_limits`, `velocity_limits`, `acceleration_limits` and `jerk_limits` with `isApprox(rhs.…, 1e-5)` and does not check their sizes first:

```cpp
bool KinematicLimits::operator==(const KinematicLimits& rhs) const
{
  bool ret_val = true;
  ret_val &= (joint_limits.isApprox(rhs.joint_limits, 1e-5));
  ret_val &= (velocity_limits.isApprox(rhs.velocity_limits, 1e-5));
  ret_val &= (acceleration_limits.isApprox(rhs.acceleration_limits, 1e-5));
  ret_val &= (jerk_limits.isApprox(rhs.jerk_limits, 1e-5));
  return ret_val;
}
```

Eigen's `isApprox` requires equal sizes and only asserts it (`eigen_assert`), so a release build compares two `MatrixX2d`s of different row counts by reading past the shorter one. Two limits for groups with different joint counts are an ordinary input, for example `KinematicLimits` from two kinematic groups.

Proposed fix: compare the four shapes first, and return `false` on any mismatch:

```cpp
auto same_shape = [](const auto& a, const auto& b) { return a.rows() == b.rows() && a.cols() == b.cols(); };
if (!same_shape(joint_limits, rhs.joint_limits) || !same_shape(velocity_limits, rhs.velocity_limits) ||
    !same_shape(acceleration_limits, rhs.acceleration_limits) || !same_shape(jerk_limits, rhs.jerk_limits))
  return false;
```

`JointState::operator==` (common/src/joint_state.cpp) already checks sizes before `isApprox`, so this matches it.

Two problems in `Environment` equality (environment/src/environment.cpp, tag 0.35.0).

**Locking.** `Environment::operator==` (:2806–2810) takes a shared lock on its own mutex only:

```cpp
bool Environment::operator==(const Environment& rhs) const
{
  std::shared_lock<std::shared_mutex> lock(mutex_);
  return std::as_const<Implementation>(*impl_) == std::as_const<Implementation>(*rhs.impl_);
}
```

`rhs.impl_` is read with no lock on `rhs.mutex_`, so comparing against an environment that another thread is modifying (`applyCommand`, `setState`) is a data race. Every other `Environment` reader takes the shared lock. Taking both locks needs a consistent order to avoid deadlock, e.g. `std::scoped_lock` over two `std::shared_lock`s, or locking by address; comparing an environment with itself must not lock twice.

**Commands compared by type only.** `Environment::Implementation::operator==` compares the command histories element by element with `*(commands[i]) == *(rhs.commands[i])` (:404–409). Both operands are `const Command&`, and `Command::operator==` is not virtual (environment/src/command.cpp:30–35):

```cpp
bool Command::operator==(const Command& rhs) const
{
  bool equal = true;
  equal &= type_ == rhs.type_;
  return equal;
}
```

So each pair is compared by `CommandType` only, and the derived commands' own `operator==` (link, joint, origin, limits, …) is never called. Two environments whose histories hold the same command types with different contents compare equal on the history. The current state and timestamps are still compared (:411–413), which usually hides this, but the history check itself is ineffective.

Proposed fix: make `Command::operator==` virtual, or dispatch on `getType()` with a `static_cast` to the derived type (the pattern `applyCommands` uses), so each command compares by value.
